Z||000000||CanadianGT||08-26-2001||06:53 PM||||Since I got my car back from the worst rebuild ever( still awaiting court date to be set) I have been trying to fix the problems on it myself.  One major one, is the battery is losing its charge when I park the car for the night, which makes me think it is a slow draw from somewhere?  Where would be a common place to check.  Another is fuel consumption.  I am only getting 300KM of driving on a full tank.  What is normal?  I was told my car was running rich by the rebuilders, but I passed my emiisions with flying coloures?  Would that test pick it up running rich?  Maybe that is not bad gas milage for a 86 GT, but im not sure.<BR>Last, but not least is my car takes about 8 to 10 seconds to turn over and finally start running.  Then sometimes when it does white smoke will come out jsut for a few seconds.  Does this mean bad valve seals already?<BR>Thanks everyone, and any help would be aprrciated.<BR>PeAcE<BR>Kyle ||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||08-26-2001||07:54 PM||||Ok  GT lets get started here the drain in your battery can be narrowed to an area by connecting a volt meter between battery cable and battery post  then go to fuse panel and start pulling fuses one at a time put them back after you pull it and see if that the particular circuit that is the culprit that is causeing the drain on the battery.when you find it you will then go to that area of the circuit and start looking for shorts in the system.oh i explained that ok.if not let me know.Next problem is your car not starting you have a fuel pump relay that turns your pump on for a few seconds then a oil presure switch takes over to make sure you have oil presure you may have faulty switch that needs to be replaced.not sure about yor gas mileage whether you have aproblem or not you need to figure out your miles per gallon and then post here what they are I dont know what is good or bad milage as I never pay much attecion to that .Hope this helps if not let us know <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||CanadianGT||08-26-2001||09:04 PM||||Thanks star for your reply, and I am going to test for thse shorts right after work tommorow.  Quick question.  I thought I remember hearing that the oil pressure switch takes over for the fuel pump relay after 2 seconds.  when my car wont start, and just turns over for like 8 to 10 seconds then finally catches, are you saying that the oil switch is not sending the signal to the pump to send gas?  would this also effect the oil pump?  Sometimes when I depress the accelerator a little it will start a little sooner?  does that sound like the switch?<BR>Thanks so much<BR>PeAcE<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||08-26-2001||09:19 PM||||Actually, GM put the oil pressure switch for the FP in there as a back-up to the relay.  The primary switch for the FP is the computer to the relay.  If your FP doesn't come on with the switch for about 2 seconds then most likely the relay is bad. After each test it will take about 10 seconds to reset so wait in between retries.  If the relay is bad then the oil switch takes a few cranks to turn on.  If this is the case watch your oil pressure gauge if you have one and the car should start when you see the gauge move.  Your short might also be in the light actuators which is protected by a fusible link and not a fuse so check those as well. GL||||reg||1
